            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Thucydides' Life and Time</strong><br>\nBorn around 460 BC in the city of Athens, Thucydides was a historian during ancient Greece's classical period. He lived through the Peloponnesian War, a conflict between Athens and Sparta, which he later chronicled. Unlike his predecessor Herodotus, who is often called the \"father of history,\" Thucydides is known for his critical approach and strict standards for evidence-based historical writing. He contracted the plague during the war but survived, an event that influenced his perspective on the conflict.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Involvement in the Peloponnesian War</strong><br>\nThucydides was more than a historian; he was also a general for Athens in the Peloponnesian War. However, after suffering a defeat at Amphipolis in 424 BC, he was exiled. This period of exile allowed him to travel widely among the warring states, gather evidence, and write his account of the war. His military experience gave him unique insights into the strategic and tactical aspects of the conflict, which enriched his historical narrative.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The History of the Peloponnesian War</strong><br>\nThe History of the Peloponnesian War is Thucydides' only known work, and it covers the war between Athens and Sparta up until 411 BC. Unfortunately, the history is incomplete, as Thucydides never finished it. Nevertheless, his work is praised for its meticulous analysis and factual accuracy. He focused on the factual reporting of events without relying on divine intervention as an explanation, which was a significant departure from the norm in historical writing of his time.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
