            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Thucydides' Methodology</strong><br>\nThucydides is often regarded as the first historian to adopt a scientific approach to history. He emphasized the importance of eyewitness accounts and direct evidence in compiling his history. Rejecting myth and legend, Thucydides sought to provide a detailed and accurate record of the Peloponnesian War, which he believed would be \"a possession for all time.\" His work was revolutionary in that he attempted to explain events in terms of human actions and natural causes rather than attributing them to the gods.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence on Historiography</strong><br>\nThucydides has been an influential figure in the field of historiography for centuries. His emphasis on impartiality, evidence, and the analysis of cause and effect has shaped the standards of historical scholarship. His legacy endures in the way history is researched and written today, with many considering him a pioneer of academic history. His methodology has also been applied to modern social sciences, demonstrating his lasting impact.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The Peloponnesian War's Impact on Athens</strong><br>\nThe Peloponnesian War had a profound effect on Athens and the whole of Greek civilization. Thucydides meticulously recorded the shifting alliances, betrayals, and the ebb and flow of Athenian and Spartan power. Through his narrative, one can understand the complexity of Greek politics and the frailty of the city-state system that eventually led to the decline of Athenian dominance and the rise of Sparta.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
