          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Realism in Political Analysis</strong><br>\nThucydides is also known for his contributions to political realism. He believed that the motivations of states and their leaders were driven by power and self-interest, rather than morality or ethics. This viewpoint has been reflected in his analysis of the Peloponnesian War, where he often discussed the pragmatic and sometimes ruthless actions of states as they sought to secure their own survival and dominance.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Thucydides and the Plague of Athens</strong><br>\nDuring the second year of the Peloponnesian War, a devastating plague struck Athens, killing a quarter of the population, including the statesman Pericles. Thucydides survived the plague and provided a detailed account of its symptoms and effects on the city. His description is one of the first clinical accounts of a disease in Western literature and is valued by epidemiologists and historians alike.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Thucydides' Enduring Legacy</strong><br>\nDespite his historical significance, little is known about Thucydides' final years or his death. He has, however, left an enduring legacy through his work, influencing countless generations with his analytical approach to history. His insistence on factual evidence and the human causes behind events remains the foundation of historical inquiry and has cemented his status as one of the greatest historians of the ancient world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
